WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.200 align:start position:19%
Way back at the dawn of aviation, during
a public flight exhibition of all

00:00:03.960 --> 00:00:10.400 align:start position:19%
things, a gull jammed a plane's
controls, causing the plane to crash and

00:00:08.440 --> 00:00:14.640 align:start position:19%
kill the pilot. Today, there are
thousands of commercial airliners and

00:00:12.600 --> 00:00:18.920 align:start position:19%
billions of birds in the sky at any
given time, so it seems like they should

00:00:16.880 --> 00:00:24.120 align:start position:19%
run into each other all the time. So,
why don't [music] birds cause more plane

00:00:21.360 --> 00:00:28.240 align:start position:19%
crashes? Hi, I'm Cameron and this is
Minute Earth. Occasionally, bird strikes

00:00:26.200 --> 00:00:32.439 align:start position:19%
[music] do bring planes down, but it's
rare enough that when it does happen,

00:00:30.280 --> 00:00:36.760 align:start position:19%
it's fodder for Hollywood movies. There
are two main reasons why birds cause so

00:00:34.560 --> 00:00:40.720 align:start position:19%
few plane crashes. The first is that
airports, where birds and planes are

00:00:38.400 --> 00:00:44.240 align:start position:19%
most likely to meet, work hard to avoid
bird strikes. [music] Airports play

00:00:42.240 --> 00:00:48.160 align:start position:19%
recordings of hawks screaming to scare
birds away from runways and unleash dogs

00:00:46.520 --> 00:00:52.000 align:start position:19%
to chase the birds when the recordings
don't work. Airport staff might even

00:00:50.200 --> 00:00:56.120 align:start position:19%
sweep runways with lasers tuned to a
wavelength known to scare birds away. On

00:00:54.280 --> 00:00:59.640 align:start position:19%
top of that, to keep birds from drinking
or bathing in tempting puddles and

00:00:57.600 --> 00:01:00.280 align:start position:19%
ponds, staff sprits the water with grape
flavoring,

00:01:00.260 --> 00:01:04.920 align:start position:19%
&gt;&gt; [music]
&gt;&gt; which weirdly birds hate. Plus, the

00:01:02.920 --> 00:01:09.080 align:start position:19%
fields around airports are seeded with
grasses birds don't like and are even

00:01:07.080 --> 00:01:12.440 align:start position:19%
mowed to a specific height, one that's
tall enough [music] to keep bug-eating

00:01:10.480 --> 00:01:16.680 align:start position:19%
birds from being able to hunt, but short
enough to keep mice from hanging out and

00:01:14.360 --> 00:01:21.720 align:start position:19%
attracting hawks. The goal here is to
simply keep birds away from planes. But,

00:01:19.120 --> 00:01:26.160 align:start position:19%
airports also use the opposite tactic,
keeping [music] planes away from birds.

00:01:23.760 --> 00:01:30.000 align:start position:19%
In 2007, airports began using radar
systems that are specially tuned to

00:01:27.960 --> 00:01:34.480 align:start position:19%
birds so controllers can tell pilots
where large flocks of birds are and how

00:01:32.160 --> 00:01:38.600 align:start position:19%
to avoid them. Airports also impose
speed limits on planes as they approach

00:01:36.440 --> 00:01:42.400 align:start position:19%
airports to, among other things, give
birds and planes the time to react if

00:01:40.560 --> 00:01:46.320 align:start position:19%
they get too close. But, there's another
big reason birds don't cause that many

00:01:44.320 --> 00:01:49.640 align:start position:19%
plane crashes. Modern planes are built
to withstand strikes when they do

00:01:48.000 --> 00:01:54.160 align:start position:19%
happen. Jet [music] plane's engines are
powerful enough that, if say some geese

00:01:52.040 --> 00:01:58.120 align:start position:19%
take out one or even two or three
engines, the plane can still climb and

00:01:56.120 --> 00:02:01.200 align:start position:19%
descend safely with a single functioning
engine. And the engines are [music]

00:01:59.280 --> 00:02:05.320 align:start position:19%
built so that if they do fail, they
don't send a shower of shrapnel into the

00:02:03.160 --> 00:02:09.640 align:start position:19%
plane itself. [music] Engineers actually
test engines by firing dead chickens

00:02:07.440 --> 00:02:13.240 align:start position:19%
directly into the rotating fan blades to
make sure that the engine casing can

00:02:11.240 --> 00:02:16.480 align:start position:19%
contain the carnage. And other critical
parts of the plane that are particularly

00:02:15.000 --> 00:02:20.440 align:start position:19%
likely to hit a bird, like the
windshield, the fronts of the wings, and

00:02:18.240 --> 00:02:25.280 align:start position:19%
the tail fin, are reinforced to
withstand [music] the impact. So today,

00:02:22.760 --> 00:02:29.680 align:start position:19%
you are almost sure to avoid that early
fliers' fate, thanks to hardworking

00:02:27.280 --> 00:02:33.360 align:start position:19%
airport staff [music] and engineers
armed with chicken guns.

